		<header class="entry-header">

			<h1 class="entry-title">Google Ads Keywords for Clinics: What to Bid On (and What&#8217;s Burning Your Budget)</h1><div class="entry-meta">By <span class="posted-by vcard author">			<a title="View all posts by Abu Bakar"
				href="/our-blog/" rel="author"
				class="url fn n"				>
				<span
				class="author-name"				>
				Abu Bakar			</span>
			</a>
		</span>

		 / <span class="posted-on"><span class="published"> August 11, 2026 </span></span></div>

		</header>

	<div class="entry-content clear"
	>

		<figure class="blog-hero"><img fetchpriority="high" width="460" height="241" decoding="async" src="/images/2026/08/clinic-google-ads-hq-cover.jpg" alt="Google Ads keywords for clinics in the UAE — targeting high-intent patient searches" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:16px;" /></figure>
<p>Two clinics run Google Ads in the same city, with the same budget, for the same treatments. One fills its consultation calendar. The other burns through thousands of dirhams and gets phone calls asking whether laser hair removal &#8220;really works.&#8221;</p>
<p>The difference is almost never the ad copy, the landing page, or the budget. It&#8217;s the keywords — specifically, whether you&#8217;re paying for <em>curiosity</em> or paying for <em>intent</em>.</p>
<h2>The intent ladder: not all searches deserve your money</h2>
<p>Every treatment your clinic offers gets searched thousands of ways, but those searches sit on very different rungs of a ladder:</p>
<p><strong>Rung 1 — Research:</strong> &#8220;what is microneedling&#8221;, &#8220;does hydrafacial hurt&#8221;, &#8220;botox side effects&#8221;. These people are learning, not booking. Great for blog content. Terrible for paid clicks.</p>
<p><strong>Rung 2 — Comparison:</strong> &#8220;hydrafacial vs chemical peel&#8221;, &#8220;best skin clinic Dubai&#8221;. Warmer, but still shopping around. Bid carefully, if at all.</p>
<p><strong>Rung 3 — Action:</strong> &#8220;hydrafacial deira price&#8221;, &#8220;botox clinic near me&#8221;, &#8220;book laser hair removal Dubai&#8221;. These searches contain a location, a price question, or a booking verb. This is where paid budgets belong.</p>
<p>The single most common mistake we see in clinic ad accounts is a campaign built on rung-1 keywords because they have the biggest search volumes. Volume is vanity. Intent pays the rent.</p>
<div class="gd-hook">
<p><strong>The rule of thumb:</strong> if you can&#8217;t imagine the searcher pulling out a credit card within 72 hours, don&#8217;t pay for the click. Answer it with content instead.</p>
</div>
<h2>The four keyword families every UAE clinic should build</h2>
<h3>1. Treatment + location</h3>
<p>The workhorse of clinic advertising: &#8220;laser hair removal Jumeirah&#8221;, &#8220;teeth whitening Abu Dhabi&#8221;, &#8220;fillers clinic Marina&#8221;. Location modifiers do two jobs — they signal booking intent and they cut out clicks from people three emirates away who will never visit you. Build one ad group per treatment-location pair so your ad and landing page can mirror the exact search.</p>
<h3>2. Treatment + price signals</h3>
<p>&#8220;Hydrafacial price Dubai&#8221;, &#8220;how much is botox in UAE&#8221;, &#8220;laser hair removal offers&#8221;. Price searchers are close to deciding. Don&#8217;t hide from these because you&#8217;re not the cheapest — a price-curious searcher who lands on a clear page explaining what&#8217;s included often books with the clinic that felt most transparent, not the one that felt cheapest.</p>
<h3>3. Problem-first keywords</h3>
<p>Patients don&#8217;t always know the treatment name; they know the problem: &#8220;acne scar treatment Dubai&#8221;, &#8220;double chin removal&#8221;, &#8220;sweating treatment underarms&#8221;. These often cost less per click than treatment-name keywords because fewer clinics think to bid on them — while the intent is just as strong.</p>
<h3>4. Competitor and brand-adjacent terms</h3>
<p>Bidding on competitor names is legal and common, but expensive and low-converting — the searcher wanted <em>them</em>, not you. If your budget is limited, this is the first family to cut. Protect your own brand name instead: it&#8217;s cheap, and it stops competitors intercepting patients who were already yours.</p>
<h2>Negative keywords: where clinic budgets quietly die</h2>
<p>The keywords you <em>block</em> matter as much as the ones you buy. Every clinic account should exclude, at minimum: &#8220;free&#8221;, &#8220;training&#8221;, &#8220;course&#8221;, &#8220;how to become&#8221;, &#8220;machine for sale&#8221;, &#8220;at home&#8221;, &#8220;DIY&#8221;, and job-seeker terms like &#8220;salary&#8221; and &#8220;vacancy&#8221;. A clinic paying for clicks from people searching &#8220;laser hair removal machine for sale&#8221; is funding someone else&#8217;s shopping trip.</p>
<p>Review your search terms report every week for the first two months. The report shows the actual phrases people typed before clicking — it&#8217;s the fastest education you&#8217;ll ever get in how your patients really search, and every irrelevant phrase you find becomes a new negative keyword.</p>
<div class="gd-checklist">
<p><strong>The clinic keyword audit — run this on your account today:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Does every paid keyword contain a location, price signal, problem, or booking verb?</li>
<li>Is each treatment in its own ad group with a matching landing page?</li>
<li>Are research-stage phrases (&#8220;what is&#8221;, &#8220;does it hurt&#8221;) excluded or moved to content?</li>
<li>Do you have at least 20 negative keywords active?</li>
<li>Have you checked the search terms report in the last 7 days?</li>
<li>Is your own brand name protected with a low-cost campaign?</li>
</ul>
</div>
<h2>Match types: keep control of what you&#8217;re actually buying</h2>
<p>Google will push you toward broad match — resist it early on. Broad match hands Google permission to show your ad for anything it considers related, and &#8220;related&#8221; in Google&#8217;s mind can be very generous with your money. Start with phrase match for your core treatment-location terms and exact match for your proven converters. Expand to broad only once you have conversion data and a solid negative list — at that point Google&#8217;s algorithm has something real to optimise toward.</p>
<h2>Keywords are half the machine</h2>
<p>Even a perfect keyword list leaks money if the click lands on a generic homepage. Every ad group needs a landing page that mirrors the search: the &#8220;hydrafacial deira&#8221; searcher should land on your Hydrafacial page with Deira mentioned, prices addressed, and a WhatsApp booking button above the fold — because <a href="/salon-whatsapp-booking-leak/">an enquiry that isn&#8217;t answered within minutes is an enquiry that books elsewhere</a>.</p>
<p>And your ads don&#8217;t exist in a vacuum: patients who click often check your reviews and your Maps listing before calling, which is why paid campaigns convert visibly better for clinics that have their <a href="/salon-not-showing-google-maps/">local visibility foundations</a> in order. For the full system — ads, local SEO, reputation and booking flow working together — our <a href="/clinic-marketing-uae-guide/">complete clinic marketing guide for the UAE</a> maps it end to end.</p>
<p>If you&#8217;d rather have specialists build and manage the machine, that&#8217;s exactly what our <a href="/google-ads-for-beauty/">Google Ads management for beauty and aesthetic clinics</a> does all day.</p>
